FINDINGS:

1. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:2508-2513 (with `interpolation_candidate` at src/lsp.rs:2829-2847) — the "member slot is still completion even when the expression after the dot is not written yet" fallback never checks that the brace pair could ever become an interpolation. `interpolation_candidate` accepts *any* balanced pair containing the caret, so a pair the parser will permanently keep as text (because its contents can never re-parse as one expression) still gets member completion. Concretely, for `object Person / has name default "Ada" / end` plus `say "{ \"a\": Person. }"`, `interpolation_at` correctly returns `None` (the inner is not an expression), but `interpolation_candidate` returns the pair, `interpolation_member_completions` re-analyzes `say  "a": Person. `, `member_slot` binds the receiver `Person` to the object, and the user is offered `name` inside a string the parser treats as JSON text. This contradicts the report's own invariant that "a pair is interpolation only when its contents re-parse as one complete expression." The new test `edge_completions_inside_balanced_braces_that_are_not_an_expression_are_empty` (tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:1020) does not cover a dot, so it never exercises the candidate branch. Fix: gate the candidate branch on the inner (with the partial member name treated as a completed identifier, or via `is_complete_expression`) being a valid interpolation, and add a JSON+receiver-dot fixture. Not run; traced the path through lines 2496–2513, 2829–2847, 2904–2906.

2. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:2496-2502 / src/lsp.rs:2917-2930 — a caret inside a *nested* text literal within a complete interpolation is treated as code. The dispatch finds the outer text token, `interpolation_at` confirms its inner text (`uppercase("hi")`) is one complete expression, and then `interpolation_completions` unconditionally offers scope names/builtins. But a caret inside the inner `"hi"` is inside a string literal and must yield nothing, exactly like `edge_completions_inside_string_literal_are_empty`. Repro: `say "x {uppercase(\"hi\")}"` with the caret on `hi` offers `greeting`/`uppercase`-style names. Fix: when the caret lies inside a quoted region of the decoded interpolation body, return an empty list. No test covers a nested literal inside a pair. Not run; traced lines 2494–2502 and 2869–2871.

3. [MINOR] src/lsp.rs:2487-2488 — the `completions` doc comment still says "Inside a string literal no code can be written, so the answer is empty", which is now false for interpolations; it will mislead the next reader about the new branch. Update it to describe the interpolation exception.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 3
